@page "/getting-started"
@using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations

<PageTitle>Getting started · Borm</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        One package, one service registration and one stylesheet - then an annotated model becomes a
        working form: labelled, validated, accessible, and handed back to you populated once it is valid.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>Bit.Borm</code> is the whole engine - the schema, the builders, JSON Schema import and export,
        the state, the rules, the components and the stylesheet - with no dependency beyond Blazor itself. It
        targets .NET 8, 9 and 10. FluentValidation support is a separate, optional package.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.Borm
<span class="c"># only if your models already have FluentValidation validators</span>
dotnet add package Bit.Borm.FluentValidation</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">Every public type lives in one namespace, prefixed <code>Borm</code>, so one line in <code>_Imports.razor</code> brings them all into scope:</p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.Borm</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Register the services</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>AddBorm()</code> registers the field renderer registry - the map from a field type to the
        component that draws it. Register it in every container that renders a form: in a Blazor Web App that
        is both the server and the client project, because every page is prerendered on the server first.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">// Program.cs - in every project that renders a form</span>
builder.Services.AddBorm();</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Link the stylesheet</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        It is not injected for you. Without it a form still works, as plain HTML; the stylesheet adds the
        theme, the grid and the error colours. There is no script to add - Borm has no JavaScript at all.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">link</span> rel=<span class="s">"stylesheet"</span> href=<span class="s">"_content/Bit.Borm/bit-borm.css"</span> /&gt;</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A first form</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Annotate a plain model and let the schema generator do the work. <code>BormView</code> builds a
        <code>BormState</code> for the definition and the model, renders every field, and calls
        <code>OnValidSubmit</code> once the submit passes validation.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">public class</span> Contact
{
    [Required, MaxLength(80)]
    <span class="k">public string</span> Name { <span class="k">get</span>; <span class="k">set</span>; } = <span class="s">""</span>;

    [Required, EmailAddress]
    <span class="k">public string</span> Email { <span class="k">get</span>; <span class="k">set</span>; } = <span class="s">""</span>;

    [Range(18, 120)]
    <span class="k">public int</span> Age { <span class="k">get</span>; <span class="k">set</span>; }
}</pre>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BormView</span> Definition=<span class="s">"_definition"</span>
          Model=<span class="s">"_model"</span>
          OnValidSubmit=<span class="s">"HandleValidSubmit"</span> /&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private readonly</span> BormDefinition _definition = BormSchemaGenerator.Generate&lt;Contact&gt;();
    <span class="k">private readonly</span> Contact _model = <span class="k">new</span>();

    <span class="k">private</span> Task HandleValidSubmit(BormState state)
    {
        <span class="c">// _model is populated and valid here</span>
        <span class="k">return</span> Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}</pre>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span></div>
        <BormView Definition="_definition" Model="_model" OnValidSubmit="HandleValidSubmit" />
        @if (_submitted is not null)
        {
            <div class="bb-note bb-note-success demo-result"><strong>Valid ✓</strong><pre>@_submitted</pre></div>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="bb-note">
        Supply a <code>State</code> instead of a <code>Definition</code> when the page wants to hold the state
        itself - to wire up a validator, read its dirty fields or drive it from code - and dispose it with the page.
        For one setting, <code>ConfigureState="s =&gt; s.ValidationTrigger = BormValidationTrigger.OnBlur"</code>
        changes the state the view builds without taking it over. With no <code>Model</code>, the form binds to a
        dictionary (<code>Data</code>) instead. Every DOM id the form renders starts with <code>borm_</code> and is
        derived from the field's path, so two forms on one page that share field names each need their own
        <code>IdPrefix</code>. Every parameter of the view is listed in the <a href="api-reference">API reference</a>.
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Hosting models</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Borm has no JavaScript and no browser-only API, so it runs the same way under every Blazor hosting model.
        A form prerenders as plain HTML and becomes interactive once the page does. A page with no interactive render
        mode at all can still use one: give the <code>BormView</code> a <code>FormName</code> and it posts like any HTML
        form and is judged on the server - see <a href="static-form">Forms without interactivity</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><strong>Blazor WebAssembly</strong> - <code>AddBorm()</code> in <code>Program.cs</code>, the stylesheet in <code>index.html</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Blazor Server</strong> - the same, in the server's <code>Program.cs</code> and <code>App.razor</code>. A field that updates on input sends every keystroke over the circuit, so prefer the default update-on-change, or a debounce, on a busy form.</li>
        <li><strong>Blazor Web App</strong> - <code>AddBorm()</code> in both the server and the client project, so both containers resolve the renderer registry.</li>
        <li><strong>Blazor Hybrid</strong> - <code>AddBorm()</code> in <code>MauiProgram.cs</code>, the stylesheet in <code>wwwroot/index.html</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Where to go next</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><a href="quickstart">From a C# type</a> - what the generator reads from a model and its attributes.</li>
        <li><a href="builder">Fluent builder + conditions</a> - typed, refactor-safe field configuration, and fields that show and hide.</li>
        <li><a href="field-types">Every field type</a> - each built-in control, and the one line of builder that asks for it.</li>
